      "content": "The historical consensus generally supports the idea that union gains created a stable economy by raising the \"floor\" for wages and reducing extreme inequality, though critics have valid points about specific employment effects in certain industries. While some argue that unions reduced aggregate demand by inflating wage bills or blocked small business adoption of labor standards, the long-term evidence suggests these criticisms often ignored the macroeconomic benefits of purchasing power and reduced economic volatility. The middle-class stability created by unions has also acted as a political safeguard against the surplus wealth that sometimes concentrates in the hands of fewer families, suggesting the \"net win\" perspective remains the most robust interpretation of the 20th-century era.",
